#>> learn
# guided tour

[ [
  [ "Operand stack"
    "  Each tab has one operand stack. Literals push themselves, and a\n  word pops its arguments and pushes its results. All arguments\n  travel this way. Effects are written ( in -- out ): + is ( a b -- c )."
    "1 2 3" ]
  [ "Postfix evaluation"
    "  Evaluation is strictly left to right, one word at a time, so\n  (2 + 3) * 4 is written 2 3 + 4 *."
    "2 3 + 4 *" ]
  [ "Stack words"
    "  dup ( a -- a a )   drop ( a -- )   swap ( a b -- b a )\n  over ( a b -- a b a )   rot ( a b c -- b c a )   nip ( a b -- b )\n  depth pushes the stack size, and empty clears the stack."
    "1 2 3 rot over" ]
  [ "Integers"
    "  Integers are signed and exact. / truncates toward zero and mod is\n  the remainder, with the sign of the left number. Hex literals\n  work, and hex formats a number back."
    "17 5 /  17 5 mod  0xff 255 hex" ]
  [ "Big numbers"
    "  Past 64 bits a number turns into a bignum by itself, so 2 100 ^\n  is exact, all 31 digits. The limit is 2^65536, about 19700\n  digits. Past that, you want omega, which comes later."
    "2 100 ^  2 64 ^ 1 -  2 1000 ^ str len" ]
  [ "Truth"
    "  Truth is a number. Comparisons leave 1 or 0, and if, when,\n  unless and while treat 0 as false and anything else as true.\n  and, or and not are logical. & | xor ~ << >> are bitwise."
    "3 5 <  4 4 = and  6 3 &" ]
  [ "Strings"
    "  Strings are immutable. cat joins, slice is ( s a n -- s ), find\n  returns an index or -1, split cuts at a separator, str and >num\n  convert to and from numbers, chr and hex format."
    "\"a,b,c\" \",\" split  \"subverge\" 3 4 slice" ]
  [ "Quotes"
    "  [ ... ] is an unevaluated sequence of values and words. It is code\n  and a list at once: do runs it, and len, at, map and append treat\n  it as data. Nested quotes are how every control structure works."
    "[ 1 2 + ] do  [ 1 2 + ] len" ]
  [ "Definitions"
    "  :name binds the top value to a name in the current tab. A quote\n  bound to a name is a word. Lookup order is locals, then this tab,\n  then root, then built-ins. forget removes a tab definition."
    "[ dup * ] :square  7 square" ]
  [ "Reflection"
    "  see prints the source of any word, including everything in root.\n  words lists this tab's definitions, and src ( 'x -- s ) returns the\n  text of the root unit that defines x."
    "'square see  words" ]
  [ "Locals"
    "  { a b } pops values into locals, with the last name on top.\n  Locals are dynamically scoped, so a callee sees its caller's.\n  ->a assigns an existing local. With no such local it makes a\n  new one that lasts until the quote or line ends. Use :a to keep it."
    "[ { a b } a b - a b + * ] :diff  9 4 diff" ]
  [ "Dynamic scope"
    "  A word sees the locals of whatever called it. That's handy, and\n  it's a trap: a helper that reads n without binding it gets its\n  caller's n, so the same word acts differently depending on who\n  calls it."
    "[ n . ] :shown  [ { n } shown ] :caller  5 caller" ]
  [ "Conditionals"
    "  if is ( flag then else -- ) and when is ( flag then -- ). The\n  branches are quotes, so they may leave different stack depths.\n  Nothing checks stack effects before the code runs."
    "7 3 > [ \"bigger\" ] [ \"smaller\" ] if" ]
  [ "Loops"
    "  n q times.  a b q for runs q with i on the stack from a to b-1.\n  [ test ] [ body ] while re-evaluates the test each pass.\n  each, map and filter walk a list."
    "1 6 [ dup * ] for  1 [ dup 100 < ] [ 2 * ] while" ]
  [ "Recursion"
    "  A word can call itself. Every level binds its own locals, and\n  only 256 fit at once, so deep recursion with locals fails.\n  Recursion that's really a loop should be a loop."
    "[ { n } n 2 < [ n ] [ n 1 - fib n 2 - fib + ] if ] :fib  20 fib" ]
  [ "Tracing"
    "  trace runs a quote one word at a time and shows the stack before\n  each step. Space steps, c runs the rest, q stops. It's the\n  closest thing omega has to a debugger."
    "[ 1 2 + 4 * ] trace" ]
  [ "Lists"
    "  range ( a b -- l ), at, first, last, rest, has and spread ( l -- ...\n  ) work on quotes. Nothing mutates: append and cat return new lists."
    "1 11 range [ 2 mod 0 = ] filter  [ 4 5 6 ] spread" ]
  [ "Transactions"
    "  Every line is a transaction on the tab's world. If any word fails,\n  the stack and the definitions revert to what they were before the\n  line. back reverts the last line that succeeded, up to 64 deep.\n  This line fails on purpose."
    "1 2 3 0 /" ]
  [ "Undo"
    "  back undoes the last line that changed your stack or your words,\n  up to 64 lines, and back back back undoes three. Lines that only\n  looked at things don't count, and redo takes a back back. Try it at\n  the prompt after learn, because learn is one long line itself."
    "" ]
  [ "Catching errors"
    "  try ( q -- 1|0 ) runs q. On failure it prints the error and leaves\n  0 instead of aborting the line. Whatever q did before the error\n  stays done: only the line as a whole is transactional."
    "[ 1 2 0 / ] try" ]
  [ "Tabs"
    "  F1 to F4 are four tabs, scheduled preemptively. Each has its own\n  stack, definitions, history and heap, and root is shared. A tab\n  stuck in a loop does not block the others. Ctrl-C stops it."
    "\"notes\" title" ]
  [ "Help"
    "  help lists the words by group. 'word help gives the effect and an\n  example, and 'group help lists a group. The table is hand written\n  in kernel/help.c, and a check fails if a word has no entry."
    "'filter help" ]
  [ "Time"
    "  ticks counts hundredths of a second since boot, 100 sleep waits\n  one second, and timeit runs a quote and says how long it took."
    "[ 100000 [ ] times ] timeit" ]
  [ "Keys"
    "  key waits for a key and gives its code. key? gives the key or -1\n  without waiting, which is how games read input. The arrows are\n  256 to 259: up, down, left, right."
    "key" ]
  [ "Drawing"
    "  text writes a string at x y in a colour from 0 to 15, put does one\n  character, and wipe clears the screen. colors shows the numbers.\n  Every game in apps is built out of these three."
    "\"hello\" 50 18 14 text" ]
  [ "Themes"
    "  The 16 colours come from a theme. themes lists them, 'raw theme\n  switches to the plain VGA colours, and 'dusk theme switches back.\n  'haelide theme also changes the logo that sys draws. logos lists\n  the logos. An installed system remembers both."
    "'haelide theme sys" ]
  [ "Ordinals"
    "  omega and epsilon0 are real values in Cantor normal form. + and *\n  are not commutative past the finite: 1 omega + is omega but\n  omega 1 + is omega+1. Subtraction past infinity is an error."
    "1 omega +  omega 1 +" ]
  [ "Cardinals"
    "  aleph0, aleph1 and continuum are cardinals. Arithmetic and\n  comparison work where ZFC decides them. Where it does not, the\n  word fails and says so. card gives the cardinality of an ordinal."
    "aleph0 2 aleph0 ^ <" ]
  [ "Counting past infinity"
    "  count walks from 0 past every finite number, through omega and\n  epsilon0, up to omega1. The other demos in apps do pairing,\n  Cantor's diagonal and a supertask. For the ideas behind\n  them, watch Vsauce's How To Count Past Infinity on YouTube."
    "omega omega * 1 +  epsilon0 1 +" ]
  [ "Same size"
    "  Two sets have the same size when their members pair off one to\n  one. pair turns two numbers into one number and unpair takes it\n  apart again. No two pairs get the same number, and no number is\n  skipped."
    "3 4 pair  32 unpair" ]
  [ "The hotel"
    "  A hotel with rooms 1, 2, 3 and so on is full. A new guest gets\n  room 1 when every guest moves up one room. hotel shows that, and\n  then seats infinitely many buses of infinitely many guests with\n  pair."
    "hotel" ]
  [ "Fractions"
    "  n rational leaves the n-th positive fraction as ( p q ). The\n  list holds every fraction once, so the fractions can be counted\n  even though there are fractions between any two of them."
    "7 rational  100 rational" ]
  [ "Too many reals"
    "  A list of infinite digit strings always misses one string:\n  change digit k of row k. So the real numbers can't be listed,\n  and 2 aleph0 ^ is larger than aleph0. diagonal draws the\n  argument."
    "aleph0 2 aleph0 ^ <" ]
  [ "Alephs"
    "  n aleph is the n-th infinite size. Adding or multiplying two\n  infinite sizes gives the larger one, and a power of two gives a\n  larger size. Whether aleph1 equals continuum can't be settled in\n  ZFC, so that comparison fails."
    "3 aleph  aleph0 aleph0 *" ]
  [ "Hailstones"
    "  n collatz counts the steps a number takes to reach 1 when even\n  numbers are halved and odd numbers become 3n+1. Nobody has found a\n  start that doesn't reach 1, and nobody has proved there is none.\n  The count is a while loop with one quote for the test and one for\n  the body."
    "27 collatz  0 8 range [ collatz ] map" ]
  [ "Bit parity"
    "  n thue is 1 when n has an odd number of 1 bits in binary, and 0\n  otherwise. In order the values are 0 1 1 0 1 0 0 1, and no block\n  of them ever occurs three times in a row. thue is a while loop on\n  & and >>."
    "0 16 range [ thue ] map" ]
  [ "Machine access"
    "  Everything runs in ring 0 with no protection. peek and poke use\n  bytes, peekd and poked use 32 bits, peekq and pokeq use 64. in and\n  out use I/O ports. alloc and free manage the heap. 0 poke works."
    "0xB8000 peek  ticks 100 /" ]
  [ "JIT"
    "  A quote that runs twice is compiled to x86-64. compile does it\n  now and leaves ( addr len ). 'name xray shows the machine code\n  next to the source, and bench times native against interpreted.\n  Editing root or redefining a built-in invalidates compiled code."
    "'square compile" ]
  [ "Programs"
    "  A program is a word. apps lists the ones that ship with the\n  system. They run in the current tab like any other word, and q\n  leaves most of them."
    "apps" ]
  [ "Small tools"
    "  clock, stopwatch, timer, todo, convert, password, ascii, colors,\n  cal and date are ordinary root words. bases ( n -- ) prints n in\n  four radixes."
    "255 bases" ]
  [ "Editing a word"
    "  'name edit opens a word you defined in scrawl, the editor. C-x C-s\n  saves, C-x C-c leaves and redefines it. For words in root, use\n  'name open instead."
    "'square edit" ]
  [ "Writing a program"
    "  A program is a quote bound to a name. It exists in this tab\n  immediately, and once the system is installed it is saved with the\n  world."
    "[ \"hello\" say ] :hello  hello" ]
  [ "Registering it in apps"
    "  apps discovers nothing. It prints app-list, a list literal in\n  root/05-apps.om, so a program appears only if you add its entry by\n  hand. In the editor, add this inside the list:\n    [ \"hello\" \"Says hello.\" ]\n  C-x C-s saves, and C-x C-c rebuilds root and leaves. If the unit does not\n  build, the old root stays and your edit is kept as a draft."
    "'apps open" ]
  [ "Checking the entry"
    "  apps reads app-list on every call, so the entry is live as soon as\n  the unit builds. If it is missing the unit did not build, and\n  'apps open shows your draft again."
    "apps" ]
  [ "Root units"
    "  A root unit is a source file linked into the image, loaded in file\n  name order. unit ( s -- ) creates a new one at the end and opens it.\n  Its words are shared by every tab and saved on installed disks.\n  In the editor, type [ \"hi\" say ] :greet, then C-x C-s and C-x C-c."
    "\"mine\" unit" ]
  [ "Key names"
    "  C- is Control and M-\n  is Alt, so C-k is Ctrl-K and M-f is Alt-F. DEL is Backspace, RET\n  is Enter and SPC is Space. C-x o is two keys, one after the\n  other. describe-key waits for a key and says what it does."
    "describe-key" ]
  [ "Moving along a line"
    "  C-a goes to the start of the line and C-e to the end. C-b and\n  C-f move one character, M-b and M-f one word. Home, End and the\n  arrows do the same. Type the line below and try the keys on it\n  before you press RET."
    "1 2 + 3 4 + * 5 6 + *" ]
  [ "Deleting text"
    "  DEL deletes before the cursor and C-d at the cursor. M-d deletes\n  the next word, M-DEL the word before, C-w the word before as\n  well, C-k everything to the end of the line and C-u everything\n  to the start. All of them except DEL and C-d keep the text for\n  the next lesson."
    "10 20 30 40 50 + + + +" ]
  [ "The kill ring"
    "  Killed text goes on the kill ring, which holds eight pieces. C-y\n  puts the last one back at the cursor, and M-y right after it\n  swaps it for the piece before. Kills that follow each other with\n  no other key between are added to one piece, so M-d M-d takes\n  two words and one C-y brings both back."
    "1 2 3 4 5 + + + +" ]
  [ "Case and swaps"
    "  C-t swaps the two characters at the cursor. M-u turns the next\n  word into capitals, M-l into small letters and M-c capitalizes\n  it. They work on any text in the line, strings included."
    "\"hello world\" upper" ]
  [ "Undo in the line"
    "  C-_ undoes the last change to the line and a second C-_ the one\n  before it. Ctrl and the slash key does the same. A run of typed\n  letters counts as one change, and a kill or a completion is one\n  change each."
    "1 2 3 + +" ]
  [ "History"
    "  C-p and C-n, or Up and Down, step through the last 16 lines of\n  the tab. M-< goes to the oldest and M-> back to the empty line.\n  Equal neighbours and empty lines are not kept. Try it at the\n  prompt when you leave learn."
    "" ]
  [ "Searching the history"
    "  C-r searches the earlier lines for the text you type, newest\n  first. A second C-r goes to the next older match and C-s turns\n  around. DEL shortens the text, C-g puts the line back as it was,\n  and any other key leaves the match in the line. RET leaves it\n  there without running it."
    "2 100 ^" ]
  [ "Completion"
    "  TAB completes the word before the cursor from your definitions,\n  root and the built-ins. One match is finished with a space,\n  several are extended as far as they share, and each further TAB\n  shows the next one. A ' or : in front of the name is kept."
    "'square see" ]
  [ "Running a word by name"
    "  M-x asks for a word on a new line, with completion, and runs it\n  as if you had typed it. The line you were editing comes back\n  afterwards. It works at the prompt and in the page viewer, so it\n  opens a program from anywhere. It is off inside learn, so try it\n  after you leave."
    "" ]
  [ "Asking about a key"
    "  C-h k waits for a key and says what it does at the prompt, in\n  the page viewer and in the editor, and shows the quote of a\n  binding of yours. describe-key is the word behind it, and it\n  works inside a lesson. Press C-k when it asks."
    "describe-key" ]
  [ "Asking about a word"
    "  C-h f asks for a word and shows its help, like 'name help. C-h a\n  asks for text and lists every word whose help mentions it, like\n  apropos. C-h i opens the help pages and C-h b the list of key\n  bindings."
    "\"line\" apropos" ]
  [ "The list of bindings"
    "  bindings, or C-h b, opens a page that lists every key at the\n  prompt, in the viewer and in the editor, and every binding of\n  yours, each with what it does. It is read like any help page,\n  and C-s searches it."
    "bindings" ]
  [ "Binding a key"
    "  bind takes a key name and a quote, and runs the quote when the\n  key is typed at the prompt. The key has to be a C- or M- key or\n  an arrow, because plain keys type text. After this line, M-j\n  types dup and a space."
    "\"M-j\" [ \"dup \" insert ] bind" ]
  [ "Editing from a binding"
    "  A binding can change the line being edited. line@ pushes the\n  line as a string and line! replaces it. point@ and point! read\n  and move the cursor, and insert types a string at the cursor.\n  They only work inside a binding. After this line, M-i turns the\n  whole line into capitals."
    "\"M-i\" [ line@ upper line! ] bind" ]
  [ "Removing a binding"
    "  unbind removes a binding of yours. A binding is a definition\n  named key: and the key, such as key:M-j, so it is saved with the\n  world of the tab, back undoes it, and a binding made in a root\n  unit works in every tab."
    "\"M-j\" unbind" ]
  [ "Key sequences"
    "  A key name can be a sequence that starts with C-x, such as C-x\n  C-t. After C-x the top bar shows C-x - and waits for the next\n  key, and C-g cancels. The editor and the window keys use the\n  same prefix."
    "\"C-x C-t\" [ \"1 2 +\" insert ] bind" ]
  [ "The page viewer"
    "  docs opens the help pages in a viewer.\n  SPC and M-v scroll, C-n and C-p move a line, l goes back, t goes\n  to the index, g asks for a page by name and q leaves. TAB\n  selects a link and RET follows it."
    "\"keys\" doc" ]
  [ "Searching a page"
    "  C-s in the viewer asks for text and jumps to the next line that\n  has it, and C-r searches backward. An empty answer repeats the\n  last search. M-x runs a word and closes the viewer, c shows the\n  credits and G the gallery."
    "\"help\" doc" ]
  [ "Moving in the editor"
    "  scrawl moves with C-f, C-b, C-n, C-p, C-a, C-e, M-f, M-b,\n  M-< and M->, C-v and M-v for a screen, and C-l to put the cursor\n  line in the middle. The arrows work as well. C-x C-c leaves."
    "'square edit" ]
  [ "Killing in the editor"
    "  C-k kills to the end of the line, and on an empty line it takes\n  the line break, so C-k twice takes a whole line. M-d and M-DEL\n  kill words and C-y yanks. Kills in a row add up, and the kill\n  buffer stays between sessions."
    "'square edit" ]
  [ "The mark and the region"
    "  C-SPC sets the mark. The text between the mark and the cursor is\n  the region. C-w kills it, M-w copies it and C-x C-x swaps the\n  cursor and the mark to show where it starts. C-g clears the\n  mark."
    "'square edit" ]
  [ "Searching the editor"
    "  C-s asks for text on the bottom row and moves to the next match,\n  C-r to the one before, and an empty answer repeats the last\n  search. M-g asks for a line number. None of them changes the\n  text."
    "'square edit" ]
  [ "Saving in the editor"
    "  C-x C-s saves and C-x C-c leaves, with C-q as an older way. With\n  unsaved changes the first C-x C-c warns and a second one leaves\n  without them. What happens to the text depends on whether it was\n  opened with edit, open or unit."
    "'square edit" ]
  [ "Windows"
    "  Up to four tabs can be on the screen at once. Super-Enter opens\n  a window, Super-W closes the one that has the keyboard and Super\n  with an arrow key moves to the window in that direction. C-x 3,\n  C-x 2, C-x 1, C-x 0 and C-x o do the same without Super."
    "" ]
  [ "Where a window goes"
    "  A new window splits the one that has the keyboard in half. It\n  goes beside it when the window is wider than it is tall as\n  drawn, and below it otherwise. The second window is beside the\n  first, the third below the second and the fourth beside the\n  third."
    "" ]
  [ "The size of a window"
    "  A tab has the size of its window. size gives the width and\n  height in characters, 80 and 24 when the tab has the whole\n  screen. The prompt, the viewer, the editor and dired follow the\n  size, and games that draw at fixed places need C-x 1."
    "size" ]
  [ "Tabs and windows"
    "  F1 to F4 show a tab in the window that has the keyboard, or move\n  to it when it already has one. Closing a window only hides its\n  tab, which keeps running. A click moves to the window under the\n  pointer."
    "mytab" ]
  [ "What phi is"
    "  phi holds named values in sets. A set holds members, and each\n  member is a value or another set, so /notes/todo is the member\n  todo of the set notes. A value is a string. phi shows what is in\n  it."
    "phi" ]
  [ "Writing a value"
    "  phi-write takes a string and a path and makes the sets on the\n  way. phi-read gives the string back. Writing to a path that\n  holds a value replaces it."
    "\"buy milk\" \"/notes/todo\" phi-write  \"/notes/todo\" phi-read" ]
  [ "Looking at a set"
    "  members lists a set, sets first, with the size of each value.\n  phi? says what a path is: 0 for nothing, 1 for a set and 2 for a\n  value."
    "\"/notes\" members  \"/notes\" phi?  \"/nothing\" phi?" ]
  [ "Moving and deleting"
    "  phi-move renames or moves a value or a set. phi-delete removes a\n  value, or a set with everything in it, and phi-set makes an\n  empty set."
    "\"/notes/todo\" \"/notes/done\" phi-move  \"/notes\" members" ]
  [ "Read only sets"
    "  /doc holds the help pages as values and /pictures the built-in\n  pictures. They can be read and listed but not changed, and\n  nothing can be added to them."
    "\"/doc/keys\" phi-read len  \"/pictures\" members" ]
  [ "dired"
    "  dired browses phi one set at a time. n and p move, RET opens a\n  set or edits a value, ^ goes up, + makes a set, c makes a value,\n  R renames, d deletes and q leaves. dired-at starts in a set."
    "dired" ]
  [ "Editing a value"
    "  phi-edit opens a value in scrawl with its path as the name. C-x\n  C-s saves and C-x C-c leaves, and the saved text becomes the\n  value. The same happens when RET is pressed on a value in dired."
    "\"/notes/done\" phi-edit" ]
  [ "phi and the disk"
    "  On an installed disk phi is written after every change, in a\n  slot of its own, so it is there after a reboot. On the live ISO\n  it starts with only /doc and /pictures. phi is not part of the\n  world of the tab, so back does not undo a write to it."
    "" ]
  [ "Pictures"
    "  gallery shows the built-in pictures: photographs, drawings and\n  screenshots of Subverge. The arrows, SPC and n go forward, p\n  goes back and q leaves. pictures lists the names."
    "pictures" ]
  [ "Credits"
    "  credits shows the people on four pages: texivi, Terry A. Davis,\n  gretagen and Vsauce. In the page viewer the c key shows it and\n  the G key shows the gallery."
    "credits" ]
  [ "The golden ratio"
    "  golden draws golden rectangles with their spiral, a sunflower of\n  seeds at the golden angle, and the digits of phi next to ratios\n  of Fibonacci numbers. SPC moves to the next view and q leaves."
    "golden" ]
  [ "Persistence"
    "  The live ISO keeps nothing. install writes the bootloader, the\n  kernel and your tabs to a disk. After that every successful line\n  and every root edit is written back. save forces a write now.\n  Use a disk you can lose."
    "" ]
  [ "Where next"
    "  This tour is root/80-learn.om, and 'learn open shows it. mandel\n  and 'mb-iter xray show the JIT on real code. 'dev help lists the\n  rest of the machine-code tools."
    "" ]
] ] :lessons

[ { i } depth list { mine }
  empty lessons len { n } 1 { going }
  [ going ] [
    lessons i at spread { ttl body task }
    wipe nocursor
    "learn  " i 1 + str cat " of " cat n str cat header
    nl 11 color "  " write ttl say 7 color nl
    body say nl
    task len 0 > [
      14 color "  Run this, or type your own line:" say 7 color
      "    " write task say nl
      empty "  > " ask { line }
      depth list { snap }
      line len 0 > [ [ line eval ] try 0 = [ empty snap spread ] when ] when
      nocursor nl
      8 color "  The stack is now:" say 7 color
      "    " write depth list dup write spread nl
    ] when
    nl 8 color "  Space next, b back, q stop." say 7 color
    key { k }
    k quit-key? [ 0 ->going ]
    [ k 98 = [ i 0 > [ i 1 - ->i ] when ] [ i n 1 - < [ i 1 + ->i ] [ 0 ->going ] if ] if ] if
  ] while
  empty
  mine spread wipe
] :lesson

[ 0 lesson ] :learn
